window.forecastDataPaper2 = [{"id":"F-LM-01","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Matrix Theory for Linear Models","subtopic":"Generalized Inverse of a Matrix","question":"If $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix of rank $r$, and $A^-$ is a generalized inverse satisfying $AA^-A=A$, what are the order of $A^-$ and the minimum possible rank of $A^-$ over all valid choices?","options":["Order $n\\times m$; minimum rank $r$","Order $m\\times n$; minimum rank $r$","Order $n\\times m$; minimum rank $\\min(m,n)$","Order $n\\times m$; minimum rank $m$"],"correctAnswer":0,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Conformability forces $A^-$ to be $n\\times m$; a reflexive g-inverse achieves the lower bound $\\operatorname{rank}(A^-)=r$.","tipsTricks":["$\\operatorname{rank}(A^-)\\ge r$ always; equality holds for reflexive g-inverses ($A^-AA^-=A^-$ too).","Conformability of $AA^-A$ pins the order of $A^-$ to $n\\times m$ whenever $A$ is $m\\times n$."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"For $AA^-A$ to be defined and equal $A$ ($m\\times n$), $A^-$ must be $n\\times m$."},{"step":2,"text":"Any g-inverse satisfies $\\operatorname{rank}(A^-)\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(A)=r$; a reflexive choice attains rank exactly $r$, the minimum possible."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (a)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-02","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Matrix Theory for Linear Models","subtopic":"Generalized Inverse of a Matrix","question":"For a symmetric matrix $A$, which statement about its generalized inverse is correct?","options":["A symmetric generalized inverse can always be found, but not every g-inverse of a symmetric matrix is symmetric","Symmetric matrices never possess a generalized inverse","A g-inverse of a symmetric matrix is symmetric only if $A$ is also idempotent","A generalized inverse of a symmetric matrix is always itself symmetric"],"correctAnswer":0,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Spectral decomposition $A=PDP'$ gives a ready-made symmetric g-inverse $PD^-P'$, but the g-inverse family is much larger and includes non-symmetric members too.","tipsTricks":["Existence of a symmetric g-inverse follows from the spectral theorem for symmetric $A$.","Uniqueness fails: the class of all g-inverses of a symmetric $A$ contains both symmetric and non-symmetric matrices."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"By the spectral theorem, $A=PDP'$ with $P$ orthogonal, $D$ diagonal; $PD^-P'$ is a symmetric g-inverse of $A$."},{"step":2,"text":"However, the defining condition $AGA=A$ does not force $G$ to be symmetric in general - other, non-symmetric $G$ also satisfy it."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (a)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-03","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Matrix Theory for Linear Models","subtopic":"Generalized Inverse of a Matrix","question":"Let $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\2&4\\end{pmatrix}$. Which of the following matrices $G$ satisfies $AGA=A$ (i.e. is a generalized inverse of $A$)?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}0.5&0\\\\0&0.5\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Numerical","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"$A$ has rank 1; test each $G$ by computing $AGA$ and checking it reproduces $A$ exactly - only option (d) survives.","tipsTricks":["For rank-deficient $A$, most 'plausible-looking' matrices fail $AGA=A$ - always verify by direct substitution.","Since $A=uu'$ with $u=(1,2)'$, any g-inverse must satisfy $u'Gu=1$; check this shortcut against each option."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"With $G=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}$: $AG=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\2&0\\end{pmatrix}$, then $(AG)A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\2&4\\end{pmatrix}=A$. Valid."},{"step":2,"text":"With $G=0.5I$: $AGA=0.5\\,A^2$; since $A^2=5A$ (as $A=uu'$, $u'u=5$), $AGA=2.5A\\ne A$. Invalid."},{"step":3,"text":"With $G=\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$: $AGA=4A\\ne A$. Invalid."},{"step":4,"text":"With $G=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}$: $AGA=9A\\ne A$. Invalid."},{"step":5,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-04","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Theory of Linear Estimation","subtopic":"Gauss-Markov Theorem, Normal Equations and BLUE","question":"Let $X_i,Y_i,Z_i$ ($i=1,2,3$) be nine independent observations with common variance $\\sigma^2$, and $E(X_i)=\\theta_1+\\theta_2$, $E(Y_i)=\\theta_1-\\theta_2$, $E(Z_i)=2\\theta_1$. Writing $X,Y,Z$ for $\\sum_{i=1}^3X_i,\\sum_{i=1}^3Y_i,\\sum_{i=1}^3Z_i$, the BLUE of $\\theta_1$ is","options":["$\\dfrac{1}{18}(2X+2Y+Z)$","$\\dfrac{1}{18}(X+Y+2Z)$","$\\dfrac{1}{9}(X+Y+Z)$","$\\dfrac{1}{18}(X-Y+2Z)$"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Numerical","difficulty":"Hard","examShortcut":"$X\\sim3(\\theta_1+\\theta_2)$, $Y\\sim3(\\theta_1-\\theta_2)$, $Z\\sim6\\theta_1$, each with variance $3\\sigma^2$ and mutually independent; minimize $a^2+b^2+c^2$ subject to unbiasedness for $\\theta_1$.","tipsTricks":["Unbiasedness forces $a=b$ (to cancel the $\\theta_2$ terms) and $6a+6c=1$.","Minimizing $2a^2+c^2$ subject to $a+c=1/6$ gives $a=1/18,\\ c=1/9$ by calculus."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"For $aX+bY+cZ$ to be unbiased for $\\theta_1$: $3(a+b+2c)\\theta_1+3(a-b)\\theta_2=\\theta_1$, so $a=b$ and $6a+6c=1$."},{"step":2,"text":"Variance $\\propto a^2+b^2+c^2=2a^2+c^2$ with $c=1/6-a$; minimizing gives $a=1/18$, hence $b=1/18$, $c=1/9$."},{"step":3,"text":"BLUE $=\\frac{1}{18}X+\\frac{1}{18}Y+\\frac{1}{9}Z=\\frac{1}{18}(X+Y+2Z)$; direct check: $E=[3(\\theta_1+\\theta_2)+3(\\theta_1-\\theta_2)+12\\theta_1]/18=\\theta_1$."},{"step":4,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-05","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Theory of Linear Estimation","subtopic":"Regression Specifics, Design Matrix, Multicollinearity","question":"For the model $y_i=\\beta_1+\\beta_2(x_i-\\alpha)$, $i=1,\\dots,n$, the least-squares estimators $\\hat\\beta_1$ and $\\hat\\beta_2$ are uncorrelated when $\\alpha$ equals","options":["$\\sum x_i^2/n$","$\\bar x$ (the sample mean of $x$)","the sample median of $x$","$0$"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"$X'X$ is diagonal exactly when $\\sum(x_i-\\alpha)=0$, i.e. $\\alpha=\\bar x$ - the standard centering trick.","tipsTricks":["Centering the regressor at its own mean is the general device for decorrelating intercept and slope estimates.","With $D(\\hat\\beta)=\\sigma^2(X'X)^{-1}$, a diagonal $X'X$ immediately gives $\\operatorname{Cov}(\\hat\\beta_1,\\hat\\beta_2)=0$."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Design matrix columns are $1$ and $(x_i-\\alpha)$; $X'X=\\begin{pmatrix}n&\\sum(x_i-\\alpha)\\\\\\sum(x_i-\\alpha)&\\sum(x_i-\\alpha)^2\\end{pmatrix}$."},{"step":2,"text":"The off-diagonal entry is $n(\\bar x-\\alpha)$, which vanishes iff $\\alpha=\\bar x$, making $X'X$ diagonal and hence $(X'X)^{-1}$ diagonal too."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-06","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Analysis of Variance","subtopic":"Two-way Classification and ANOVA","question":"In a two-way classification with interaction, factor $A$ at 3 levels, factor $B$ at 4 levels, and 5 observations per cell, the error degrees of freedom equal","options":["12","44","48","59"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Numerical","difficulty":"Easy","examShortcut":"Error df $=ab(m-1)=3\\times4\\times4=48$.","tipsTricks":["Total df $=abm-1=59$ splits as $A$:2, $B$:3, $AB$:6, Error:48 - these sum to 59, a useful check.","Error df in a replicated two-way design is always $ab(m-1)$, independent of how $A,B$ df are computed."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$N=abm=3\\times4\\times5=60$ total observations."},{"step":2,"text":"Error df $=N-ab=60-12=48$, equivalently $ab(m-1)=12\\times4=48$."},{"step":3,"text":"Check: $2+3+6+48=59=N-1$. Consistent."},{"step":4,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-07","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Analysis of Variance","subtopic":"One-way Classification and ANOVA","question":"In a one-way ANOVA with 4 treatments and 5 observations per treatment, Treatment SS $=45$ and Total SS $=105$. The value of the $F$ statistic is","options":["5.00","6.00","3.00","4.00"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Numerical","difficulty":"Easy","examShortcut":"Error SS $=105-45=60$; $F=(45/3)/(60/16)=15/3.75=4$.","tipsTricks":["Treatment df $=k-1=3$; Error df $=N-k=16$ (since $N=20$).","Always subtract Treatment SS from Total SS to get Error SS before forming the $F$ ratio."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$N=20$; Treatment df $=3$, Error df $=16$; Error SS $=105-45=60$."},{"step":2,"text":"MS(Treatment)$=45/3=15$; MS(Error)$=60/16=3.75$."},{"step":3,"text":"$F=15/3.75=4.00$."},{"step":4,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-08","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Theory of Linear Estimation","subtopic":"Estimable Functions, Estimation and Error Space","question":"In the Gauss-Markov model $Y=X\\beta+e$, a linear parametric function $\\lambda'\\beta$ is estimable if and only if","options":["$X$ has full column rank","$\\lambda'\\lambda=1$","$\\lambda$ lies in the null space of $X$","$\\lambda'=a'X$ for some vector $a$ (i.e. $\\lambda$ lies in the row space of $X$)"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Estimability is exactly membership of $\\lambda'$ in the row space of $X$ (equivalently, of $X'X$).","tipsTricks":["When $X$ is full column rank, EVERY $\\lambda'\\beta$ is estimable, since the row space of $X$ is then all of $\\mathbb R^p$.","This condition does not require normality or independence of errors - it is purely a linear-algebra fact about $X$."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$\\lambda'\\beta$ is estimable iff there is a linear unbiased estimator $a'Y$ for it, i.e. $E(a'Y)=a'X\\beta=\\lambda'\\beta$ for all $\\beta$, i.e. $a'X=\\lambda'$."},{"step":2,"text":"This says exactly that $\\lambda'$ is a linear combination of the rows of $X$ - the row space condition."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-09","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Theory of Linear Estimation","subtopic":"Gauss-Markov Theorem, Normal Equations and BLUE","question":"For the model $Y=X\\beta+e$ with possibly singular $X'X$, if $G$ is any generalized inverse of $X'X$, then $XGX'X$ equals","options":["the identity matrix","the zero matrix","$X'X$","$X$, for every choice of generalized inverse $G$"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"$XGX'X=X$ for any g-inverse $G$ of $X'X$ - the key lemma behind invariance of fitted values $\\hat Y=X\\hat\\beta$ to the choice of $G$.","tipsTricks":["This is why $\\hat\\beta=G X'Y$ can differ across choices of $G$, yet $X\\hat\\beta$ (and every estimable function) stays the same.","The lemma follows from $\\operatorname{rank}(X'X)=\\operatorname{rank}(X)$ and a standard g-inverse identity."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"This is a standard lemma of generalized-inverse theory for normal equations: whenever $G$ satisfies $(X'X)G(X'X)=X'X$, it follows that $XGX'X=X$."},{"step":2,"text":"Consequently fitted values and all estimable functions of $\\beta$ are invariant to the choice of $G$ used to solve $X'X\\beta=X'Y$."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-10","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Theory of Linear Estimation","subtopic":"Variances and Covariances of LSEs; Error Variance","question":"In simple linear regression with $n=5$, $\\bar x=3$, $\\sum(x_i-\\bar x)^2=20$ and error variance $\\sigma^2=4$, $\\operatorname{Cov}(\\hat\\beta_0,\\hat\\beta_1)$ equals","options":["$0.6$","$-0.6$","$0$","$-1.2$"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Numerical","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"$\\operatorname{Cov}(\\hat\\beta_0,\\hat\\beta_1)=-\\sigma^2\\bar x/\\sum(x_i-\\bar x)^2=-4(3)/20=-0.6$.","tipsTricks":["The covariance is always $-\\sigma^2\\bar x/S_{xx}$ - note the negative sign, a frequent trap.","It vanishes only when $\\bar x=0$, matching the centering result used elsewhere in this section."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$\\operatorname{Cov}(\\hat\\beta_0,\\hat\\beta_1)=-\\sigma^2\\bar x/\\sum(x_i-\\bar x)^2=-4\\times3/20=-12/20=-0.6$."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-11","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Matrix Theory for Linear Models","subtopic":"Hat Matrix, Residuals and Quadratic Forms","question":"For the hat matrix $H=X(X'X)^{-1}X'$ in a full-rank regression model, which statement is FALSE?","options":["$\\operatorname{Cov}(e_i,e_j)=-\\sigma^2h_{ij}$ for $i\\ne j$","$\\operatorname{Cov}(e_i,e_j)=+\\sigma^2h_{ij}$ for $i\\ne j$","$\\sum_ih_{ii}=p$, the number of parameters","$0\\le h_{ii}\\le1$ for every $i$"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Residual covariance carries a MINUS sign: $\\operatorname{Cov}(e_i,e_j)=-\\sigma^2h_{ij}$; the '+' version is the planted false statement.","tipsTricks":["$D(e)=\\sigma^2(I-H)$, so off-diagonal entries are $-\\sigma^2h_{ij}$, never positive $h_{ij}$.","$h_{ii}\\in[0,1]$ and $\\sum h_{ii}=\\operatorname{tr}(H)=\\operatorname{rank}(H)=p$ are both standard, true facts."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Since $e=(I-H)Y$ has $D(e)=\\sigma^2(I-H)$, the $(i,j)$ entry for $i\\ne j$ is $-\\sigma^2h_{ij}$, not $+\\sigma^2h_{ij}$."},{"step":2,"text":"The other three statements ($h_{ii}\\in[0,1]$, $\\sum h_{ii}=p$) are standard true properties of the idempotent projection $H$."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (b) is false."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-12","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Analysis of Variance","subtopic":"Fixed, Random and Mixed Effects Models","question":"In a two-way ANOVA with both factors random and replication present, testing $H_0:$ no main effect of factor $A$ requires the $F$-test denominator to be","options":["MSE, the error mean square","MS(B)","the total mean square","MS(AB), the interaction mean square"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Hard","examShortcut":"Under the random-effects model, $E[\\text{MS}(A)]=\\sigma_e^2+m\\sigma_{AB}^2+bm\\sigma_A^2$ and $E[\\text{MS}(AB)]=\\sigma_e^2+m\\sigma_{AB}^2$, so $F=\\text{MS}(A)/\\text{MS}(AB)$ isolates $\\sigma_A^2$.","tipsTricks":["Testing the interaction itself DOES use MSE as the denominator, since $E[\\text{MSE}]=\\sigma_e^2$ alone.","Using MSE (instead of MS(AB)) to test a random main effect is a classic error - it overstates significance."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Expected mean squares under the random-effects model show MS(A) and MS(AB) share the term $m\\sigma_{AB}^2$, differing only in the $\\sigma_A^2$ contribution."},{"step":2,"text":"Hence $F=\\text{MS}(A)/\\text{MS}(AB)$ is the correct test for $H_0:\\sigma_A^2=0$; using MSE instead would inflate the Type I error rate."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-13","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Theory of Linear Estimation","subtopic":"Regression Specifics, Design Matrix, Multicollinearity","question":"For nested linear models where Model 1 (with $p_1$ parameters) is a special case of Model 2 (with $p_2>p_1$ parameters), both fitted to the same data by least squares, which is necessarily true of the residual sums of squares?","options":["$\\text{RSS}_1=\\text{RSS}_2$ always","$\\text{RSS}_1<\\text{RSS}_2$ always","$\\text{RSS}_1\\ge\\text{RSS}_2$ always, and $\\text{RSS}_1-\\text{RSS}_2=0$ is possible even when $p_2>p_1$","$\\text{RSS}_1-\\text{RSS}_2$ can be negative"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Model 2's larger parameter space contains Model 1's fit as a special case, so its minimized RSS can only be $\\le$ Model 1's.","tipsTricks":["Equality $\\text{RSS}_1=\\text{RSS}_2$ occurs precisely when the extra parameters' least-squares estimates are exactly zero (or otherwise redundant for this data) - a measure-zero but real event.","This monotonicity is the basis of the partial-$F$ test for nested models."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Since Model 1 is a restriction of Model 2, any fit achievable under Model 1 is also achievable under Model 2; therefore Model 2's minimum RSS cannot exceed Model 1's."},{"step":2,"text":"So $\\text{RSS}_1\\ge\\text{RSS}_2$ always, and the gap $Q=\\text{RSS}_1-\\text{RSS}_2\\ge0$ can still be exactly $0$ for particular data even when $p_2>p_1$."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-14","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Theory of Linear Estimation","subtopic":"Correlated Observations, Weighted LS and GLS","question":"In the model $Y=X\\beta+e$ with $D(e)=\\sigma^2G$ ($G$ known, positive definite), transforming $Z=G^{-1/2}Y$ reduces the model to Gauss-Markov form. The resulting GLS estimator of $\\beta$ is","options":["$(X'X)^{-1}X'Y$","$(X'GX)^{-1}X'GY$","$(X'G^{-1}X)^{-1}X'G^{-1}Y$","$(X'G^2X)^{-1}X'G^2Y$"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Hard","examShortcut":"OLS on the transformed model $Z=WY,\\ W=G^{-1/2}X$ gives $(W'W)^{-1}W'Z=(X'G^{-1}X)^{-1}X'G^{-1}Y$ since $G^{-1/2}{}'G^{-1/2}=G^{-1}$.","tipsTricks":["GLS is just OLS after the whitening transform $G^{-1/2}$; it is unbiased and BLUE under the transformed Gauss-Markov assumptions.","Never use $G$ or $G^2$ in place of $G^{-1}$ in the GLS formula - a very common transcription error."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Under $Z=G^{-1/2}Y$, $W=G^{-1/2}X$, the transformed errors $G^{-1/2}e$ have covariance $\\sigma^2 G^{-1/2}GG^{-1/2}{}'=\\sigma^2I$."},{"step":2,"text":"OLS on $(W,Z)$ gives $\\hat\\beta=(W'W)^{-1}W'Z=(X'G^{-1}X)^{-1}X'G^{-1}Y$."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-15","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Analysis of Variance","subtopic":"Multiple Comparison Tests (Tukey, Scheffe, SNK-Duncan)","question":"Which multiple comparison procedure uses the studentized range distribution and a SINGLE fixed critical value for all pairwise comparisons among treatment means (with equal sample sizes)?","options":["Bonferroni's method","Student-Newman-Keuls (SNK) test","Tukey's HSD test","Scheffe's test"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Factual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Tukey's HSD: one studentized-range critical value applied uniformly to all pairwise differences - the most standard equal-$n$ pairwise procedure.","tipsTricks":["SNK also uses the studentized range but with STEP-WISE critical values that change with the number of means spanned - not a single fixed value.","Scheffe's test is more conservative and valid for ANY contrast, not just pairwise comparisons, using the $F$ distribution instead."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Tukey's HSD applies one critical value, $q_{\\alpha,k,\\nu}\\cdot\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}/n}$, to every pairwise comparison - simple, uniform, and standard for equal-$n$ designs."},{"step":2,"text":"SNK varies the critical value with the 'span' of means being compared; Scheffe covers arbitrary contrasts at the cost of power for simple pairwise tests."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-16","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Theory of Linear Estimation","subtopic":"Correlated Observations, Weighted LS and GLS","question":"In the heteroscedastic model $y_i=\\beta x_i+e_i$ (no intercept) with $\\operatorname{Var}(e_i)=\\sigma^2/w_i$ for known weights $w_i$, the weighted least squares estimator of $\\beta$ is","options":["$\\dfrac{\\sum w_ix_iy_i}{\\sum w_ix_i^2}$","$\\dfrac{\\sum y_i}{\\sum x_i}$","$\\dfrac{\\sum x_iy_i}{\\sum x_i^2}$","$\\dfrac{\\sum w_iy_i}{\\sum w_ix_i}$"],"correctAnswer":0,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Minimizing $\\sum w_i(y_i-\\beta x_i)^2$ and setting the derivative to zero gives $\\hat\\beta=\\sum w_ix_iy_i/\\sum w_ix_i^2$.","tipsTricks":["WLS weights each squared residual by $w_i$ (inversely proportional to its variance), down-weighting noisier observations.","Setting all $w_i=1$ recovers the ordinary (unweighted) least-squares slope, a useful sanity check."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Minimize $S(\\beta)=\\sum w_i(y_i-\\beta x_i)^2$; $dS/d\\beta=-2\\sum w_ix_i(y_i-\\beta x_i)=0$."},{"step":2,"text":"$\\Rightarrow \\sum w_ix_iy_i=\\beta\\sum w_ix_i^2 \\Rightarrow \\hat\\beta=\\sum w_ix_iy_i/\\sum w_ix_i^2$."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (a)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-17","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Matrix Theory for Linear Models","subtopic":"Hat Matrix, Residuals and Quadratic Forms","question":"If $P$ is an idempotent matrix ($P^2=P$) of order $n\\times n$ with rank $r$, its eigenvalues are","options":["All equal to $1$","All equal to $r/n$","All $0$ or $1$, with exactly $r$ eigenvalues equal to $1$","All equal to $\\pm1$"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Idempotency forces eigenvalues to satisfy $\\lambda^2=\\lambda$, so $\\lambda\\in\\{0,1\\}$; the count of $1$'s equals $\\operatorname{tr}(P)=\\operatorname{rank}(P)=r$.","tipsTricks":["This underlies why $\\sum h_{ii}=p$ for the hat matrix - it is exactly this eigenvalue-counting fact applied to $H$.","Any eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of $P$ satisfies $Pv=\\lambda v\\Rightarrow P^2v=\\lambda^2v=Pv=\\lambda v\\Rightarrow\\lambda^2=\\lambda$."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"If $Pv=\\lambda v$, then $P^2v=\\lambda^2v$; but $P^2=P$ gives $P^2v=Pv=\\lambda v$, so $\\lambda^2=\\lambda$, forcing $\\lambda\\in\\{0,1\\}$."},{"step":2,"text":"Since $\\operatorname{tr}(P)=\\sum\\lambda_i=\\operatorname{rank}(P)=r$ for an idempotent matrix, exactly $r$ of the eigenvalues equal $1$ and the rest are $0$."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-18","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Theory of Linear Estimation","subtopic":"Regression Specifics, Design Matrix, Multicollinearity","question":"For the design matrix $X$ ($n\\times p$) in a linear model, $\\operatorname{rank}(X'X)$ equals","options":["$\\operatorname{rank}(X)$","$\\min(n,p)$, regardless of $X$","$p$, always","$n$, always"],"correctAnswer":0,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Easy","examShortcut":"$\\operatorname{rank}(X'X)=\\operatorname{rank}(X)$ is a standard linear-algebra identity, true whether or not $X$ has full column rank.","tipsTricks":["This identity is exactly why solving the normal equations via a generalized inverse works even when $X$ is rank-deficient.","It follows because $X$ and $X'X$ have the same null space: $Xv=0\\Leftrightarrow X'Xv=0$."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$Xv=0\\Rightarrow X'Xv=0$ trivially; conversely $X'Xv=0\\Rightarrow v'X'Xv=0\\Rightarrow\\|Xv\\|^2=0\\Rightarrow Xv=0$."},{"step":2,"text":"So $X$ and $X'X$ share the same null space, hence the same rank."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (a)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-19","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Theory of Linear Estimation","subtopic":"Gauss-Markov Theorem, Normal Equations and BLUE","question":"When $X$ has full column rank $p$, the Gauss-Markov (BLUE) estimator of $\\beta$ itself is","options":["non-unique even though $X'X$ is invertible","unique only for estimable functions, not for $\\beta$ itself","unique, equal to $(X'X)^{-1}X'Y$","undefined"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Easy","examShortcut":"Full column rank makes $X'X$ invertible, so every $\\lambda'\\beta$ (including $\\beta$ itself) is estimable with a unique BLUE $(X'X)^{-1}X'Y$.","tipsTricks":["Only in the RANK-DEFICIENT case does one need to fall back on 'only estimable functions have unique BLUEs' - full rank sidesteps that entirely.","This is the standard, simplest form of the Gauss-Markov theorem taught first, before the generalized-inverse extension."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"With $X$ full column rank, $X'X$ is nonsingular, so the normal equations $X'X\\beta=X'Y$ have the unique solution $\\hat\\beta=(X'X)^{-1}X'Y$, which is BLUE by Gauss-Markov."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-20","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Theory of Linear Estimation","subtopic":"Variances and Covariances of LSEs; Error Variance","question":"In a simple linear regression with error df $=10$, $\\hat\\beta_1=2.5$, $SE(\\hat\\beta_1)=0.5$, and $t_{0.025,10}=2.228$, a 95% confidence interval for $\\beta_1$ is","options":["$(1.39,\\ 3.61)$","$(0.50,\\ 4.50)$","$(2.00,\\ 3.00)$","$(1.50,\\ 3.50)$"],"correctAnswer":0,"questionType":"Numerical","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"$2.5\\pm2.228(0.5)=2.5\\pm1.114=(1.386,\\ 3.614)$.","tipsTricks":["Always use the $t$ critical value at the ERROR degrees of freedom, not $n-1$, for regression coefficient CIs.","Half-width $=t\\times SE$; add/subtract from the point estimate."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Half-width $=2.228\\times0.5=1.114$."},{"step":2,"text":"CI $=2.5\\pm1.114=(1.386,\\ 3.614)\\approx(1.39,\\ 3.61)$."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (a)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-21","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Theory of Linear Estimation","subtopic":"Regression Specifics, Design Matrix, Multicollinearity","question":"Under severe multicollinearity among the regressors (with $X$ still of full column rank), the OLS estimator $\\hat\\beta=(X'X)^{-1}X'Y$ remains","options":["completely unaffected","unbiased, but with inflated variance and unstable estimates","no longer a linear estimator","biased and inconsistent"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Gauss-Markov only needs full rank, not a well-conditioned $X'X$; multicollinearity inflates $(X'X)^{-1}$'s diagonal (variance) without breaking unbiasedness.","tipsTricks":["Large standard errors and sign instability across resamples are the practical symptoms of this variance inflation.","Ridge regression deliberately trades a little bias for a large reduction in this variance."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Unbiasedness and the Gauss-Markov BLUE property depend only on $X$ having full column rank, which multicollinearity (short of exact linear dependence) does not violate."},{"step":2,"text":"However, near-singular $X'X$ makes $(X'X)^{-1}$ have very large entries, inflating $\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat\\beta_j)=\\sigma^2[(X'X)^{-1}]_{jj}$."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-22","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Theory of Linear Estimation","subtopic":"Gauss-Markov Theorem, Normal Equations and BLUE","question":"The Gauss-Markov theorem states that, under $E(e)=0$, $D(e)=\\sigma^2I$, the least-squares estimator of any estimable $\\lambda'\\beta$ is","options":["the MLE, which requires a normality assumption","BLUE among all linear unbiased estimators, without requiring normality","unbiased only, with no minimum-variance guarantee","the UMVUE among ALL unbiased estimators, not just linear ones"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Gauss-Markov's guarantee is BLUE - best among LINEAR unbiased estimators - and needs no distributional assumption at all.","tipsTricks":["Upgrading BLUE to full UMVUE (among ALL unbiased estimators) needs normality plus a completeness argument via Lehmann-Scheffe - a strictly stronger claim.","This distinction (BLUE vs UMVUE) is a favourite conceptual trap in this topic."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Gauss-Markov compares the LSE only against other LINEAR unbiased estimators and shows it has minimum variance in that restricted class - hence 'BLUE'."},{"step":2,"text":"No normality is assumed or needed; normality is what additionally makes the LSE the UMVUE among all (not just linear) unbiased estimators."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-23","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Matrix Theory for Linear Models","subtopic":"Hat Matrix, Residuals and Quadratic Forms","question":"If $Y\\sim N_n(X\\beta,\\sigma^2I)$ and $H=X(X'X)^{-1}X'$ has rank $p$, then $(Y-X\\hat\\beta)'(Y-X\\hat\\beta)/\\sigma^2$ follows","options":["$\\chi^2$ with $p$ degrees of freedom","an $F$ distribution with $(p,n-p)$ degrees of freedom","$\\chi^2$ with $(n-p)$ degrees of freedom","$\\chi^2$ with $n$ degrees of freedom"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Hard","examShortcut":"Residual SS $=e'e=Y'(I-H)Y$ with $(I-H)$ idempotent of rank $n-p$, giving $\\chi^2_{n-p}$ under normality.","tipsTricks":["$(I-H)X=0$, so $Y-X\\hat\\beta=(I-H)Y=(I-H)e$, and $(I-H)$'s rank ($=n-p$) is exactly the chi-square degrees of freedom.","This result underlies every $t$- and $F$-test built from regression residuals."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$Y-X\\hat\\beta=(I-H)Y$; since $(I-H)$ is symmetric idempotent with rank $n-p$, the standard quadratic-form theorem gives $(Y-X\\hat\\beta)'(Y-X\\hat\\beta)/\\sigma^2\\sim\\chi^2_{n-p}$."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-24","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Analysis of Variance","subtopic":"One-way Classification and ANOVA","question":"In one-way ANOVA testing equality of $k$ treatment means from $N$ total observations, the statistic $F=\\text{MST}/\\text{MSE}$ follows, under $H_0$, the distribution","options":["$F_{N-1,\\,N-k}$","$F_{N-k,\\,k-1}$","$F_{k-1,\\,N-k}$","$F_{k,\\,N-1}$"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Easy","examShortcut":"Numerator df = treatment df = $k-1$; denominator df = error df = $N-k$.","tipsTricks":["The numerator is always the 'smaller', more restrictive source (Treatment); mixing up numerator/denominator df is the classic slip.","This holds regardless of whether group sizes are equal, as long as $N=\\sum n_i$."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"MST has $k-1$ df; MSE has $N-k$ df; their ratio under $H_0$ follows $F_{k-1,N-k}$ by Cochran's theorem."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-LM-25","section":"Linear Models","topic":"Analysis of Variance","subtopic":"Multiple Comparison Tests (Tukey, Scheffe, SNK-Duncan)","question":"Compared to Tukey's HSD, Scheffe's method for multiple comparisons is","options":["valid only when comparing exactly two treatments","always identical to Tukey's HSD","less conservative than Tukey's HSD for pairwise comparisons","more conservative for simple pairwise comparisons, but valid for testing ANY linear contrast, not just pairwise ones"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Factual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Scheffe controls the family-wise error rate over the INFINITE family of all contrasts, so it must be wider (more conservative) than Tukey for the narrower pairwise-only case.","tipsTricks":["Use Tukey when only pairwise comparisons are of interest (it is more powerful there); use Scheffe when arbitrary contrasts (not just pairs) are to be tested.","Scheffe's critical value is based on the $F$ distribution, not the studentized range."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Because Scheffe's method must simultaneously protect against every possible linear contrast (an uncountable family), its critical value is necessarily larger than Tukey's for the pairwise-only case, making it more conservative there - but uniquely valid for post-hoc contrasts Tukey cannot cover."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-01","section":"Estimation","topic":"Point Estimation","subtopic":"Characteristics of a Good Estimator (Unbiasedness, Consistency, Efficiency, MSE)","question":"An estimator $T_n$ of $\\theta$ is said to be (weakly) consistent if","options":["$P(|T_n-\\theta|>\\varepsilon)\\to0$ as $n\\to\\infty$, for every $\\varepsilon>0$","$\\operatorname{Var}(T_n)=0$ for every $n$","$E(T_n)=\\theta$ for every $n$","$T_n=\\theta$ exactly for all sufficiently large $n$"],"correctAnswer":0,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Easy","examShortcut":"Consistency is convergence in probability of $T_n$ to $\\theta$ - a large-sample property, distinct from (and not implied by) unbiasedness.","tipsTricks":["An estimator can be consistent yet biased for every finite $n$ (bias $\\to0$ is enough), and unbiased yet inconsistent (variance not $\\to0$).","Do not confuse this with almost-sure convergence (strong consistency) - the exam usually intends the weak/probability version."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"By definition, $T_n\\xrightarrow{P}\\theta$ means for every $\\varepsilon>0$, $P(|T_n-\\theta|>\\varepsilon)\\to0$ as $n\\to\\infty$."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (a)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-02","section":"Estimation","topic":"Point Estimation","subtopic":"Characteristics of a Good Estimator (Unbiasedness, Consistency, Efficiency, MSE)","question":"For an estimator $T$ of $\\theta$, the mean squared error $\\text{MSE}(T)=E[(T-\\theta)^2]$ decomposes as","options":["$\\operatorname{Var}(T)-[\\text{Bias}(T)]^2$","$\\operatorname{Var}(T)/\\text{Bias}(T)$","$[\\operatorname{Var}(T)]^2+\\text{Bias}(T)$","$\\operatorname{Var}(T)+[\\text{Bias}(T)]^2$"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Easy","examShortcut":"The bias-variance decomposition: $\\text{MSE}=\\operatorname{Var}(T)+[\\text{Bias}(T)]^2$.","tipsTricks":["For an unbiased estimator, MSE reduces to just the variance - this is why unbiased comparisons focus purely on variance.","A biased estimator can still have LOWER MSE than an unbiased one if it has substantially smaller variance."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$E[(T-\\theta)^2]=E[(T-E(T)+E(T)-\\theta)^2]=\\operatorname{Var}(T)+[\\text{Bias}(T)]^2$ (cross term vanishes)."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-03","section":"Estimation","topic":"Point Estimation","subtopic":"Characteristics of a Good Estimator (Unbiasedness, Consistency, Efficiency, MSE)","question":"Regarding minimization of MSE over ALL estimators (not just unbiased ones), which statement is correct?","options":["The sample mean always has the smallest MSE among all estimators of the population mean","Minimizing MSE and requiring unbiasedness are equivalent conditions","The MLE always has the smallest MSE for every $\\theta$","No single estimator achieves uniformly minimum MSE over all $\\theta$ among all estimators - restricting to unbiased estimators is necessary for a meaningful 'best estimator' theory"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"The trivial constant estimator $T\\equiv\\theta_0$ has $\\text{MSE}=(\\theta_0-\\theta)^2=0$ exactly at $\\theta=\\theta_0$, so no estimator can beat it everywhere - this is why UMVUE theory restricts attention to unbiased estimators.","tipsTricks":["This is precisely why 'uniformly minimum variance UNBIASED estimator' (UMVUE), not 'uniformly minimum MSE estimator', is the object of interest in estimation theory.","A silly constant guess always wins the MSE race at exactly one point - hence no universal winner exists without the unbiasedness restriction."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Consider $T\\equiv\\theta_0$ (ignore the data, always guess $\\theta_0$): its MSE is $0$ when $\\theta=\\theta_0$, beating every other estimator there."},{"step":2,"text":"Since this holds for every choice of $\\theta_0$, no single estimator can have uniformly (for all $\\theta$) minimum MSE among ALL estimators - the restriction to unbiased estimators removes this pathology."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-04","section":"Estimation","topic":"Methods of Estimation","subtopic":"Maximum Likelihood Estimation","question":"For $X_1,\\dots,X_n$ iid $U(0,\\theta)$, the maximum likelihood estimator of $\\theta$ is","options":["$X_{(1)}$, the smallest order statistic","$X_{(n)}$, the largest order statistic","$2\\bar X$","$\\bar X$"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Likelihood $=\\theta^{-n}$ for $\\theta\\ge X_{(n)}$, strictly decreasing in $\\theta$ - so the MLE is the smallest admissible $\\theta$, namely $X_{(n)}$.","tipsTricks":["$2\\bar X$ is the method-of-moments estimator for this family, NOT the MLE - a very common mix-up.","Whenever the support depends on $\\theta$, differentiating the log-likelihood is invalid; reason directly from the likelihood's shape instead."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$f(x,\\theta)=1/\\theta$ for $0\\le x\\le\\theta$, so $L(\\theta)=\\theta^{-n}$ provided $\\theta\\ge\\max(x_i)=X_{(n)}$, and $L=0$ otherwise."},{"step":2,"text":"$L(\\theta)$ is decreasing in $\\theta$ over its valid range, so it is maximized at the smallest permissible $\\theta$, i.e. $\\hat\\theta_{MLE}=X_{(n)}$."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-05","section":"Estimation","topic":"Methods of Estimation","subtopic":"Maximum Likelihood Estimation","question":"For $X_1,\\dots,X_n$ iid $U(\\theta-\\tfrac12,\\theta+\\tfrac12)$, the maximum likelihood estimator of $\\theta$","options":["is uniquely $\\bar X$","does not exist","is not unique - any value in $[X_{(n)}-\\tfrac12,\\ X_{(1)}+\\tfrac12]$ maximizes the likelihood equally","is uniquely the midrange $(X_{(1)}+X_{(n)})/2$"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Hard","examShortcut":"The likelihood is constant ($=1$) for every $\\theta$ satisfying $X_{(n)}-\\tfrac12\\le\\theta\\le X_{(1)}+\\tfrac12$, so the MLE is non-unique - the midrange is merely one conventional choice among infinitely many.","tipsTricks":["This is a classic 'the MLE need not be unique' example, distinct from the earlier $U(0,\\theta)$ case where it IS unique.","The midrange is popular because it is also the midpoint of the valid interval, but any point of that interval is equally valid as 'an' MLE."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"The likelihood equals $1$ (constant) as long as every $x_i$ lies in $[\\theta-\\tfrac12,\\theta+\\tfrac12]$, i.e. $\\theta\\ge X_{(n)}-\\tfrac12$ and $\\theta\\le X_{(1)}+\\tfrac12$."},{"step":2,"text":"Any $\\theta$ in this interval gives the same (maximal) likelihood value, so the MLE set is the whole interval, not a single point."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-06","section":"Estimation","topic":"Methods of Estimation","subtopic":"Method of Moments","question":"An iid sample from $U(a,b)$ has $\\bar X=10$ and $\\frac1n\\sum(X_i-\\bar X)^2=12$. The method-of-moments estimates of $a$ and $b$ are","options":["$a=0,\\ b=20$","$a=4,\\ b=16$","$a=8,\\ b=12$","$a=-2,\\ b=22$"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Numerical","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"$a+b=2\\bar X=20$; $b-a=2\\sqrt{3\\times12}=2\\sqrt{36}=12$; solving gives $a=4,b=16$.","tipsTricks":["MoM for $U(a,b)$ always produces the tell-tale factor $2\\sqrt3$ multiplying the sample standard deviation for $b-a$.","Always solve the pair of equations $a+b=2\\bar X$, $b-a=2\\sqrt{3m_2}$ simultaneously - don't average or guess."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Mean: $(a+b)/2=\\bar X=10\\Rightarrow a+b=20$."},{"step":2,"text":"Variance: $(b-a)^2/12=m_2=12\\Rightarrow (b-a)^2=144\\Rightarrow b-a=12$."},{"step":3,"text":"Solving: $b=16,\\ a=4$."},{"step":4,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-07","section":"Estimation","topic":"Methods of Estimation","subtopic":"Method of Moments","question":"For $X_1,\\dots,X_n$ iid Exponential with rate $\\theta$ (mean $1/\\theta$), do the method-of-moments and maximum-likelihood estimators of $\\theta$ coincide?","options":["No, they differ by a factor of $n/(n-1)$","Only asymptotically, not for finite $n$","No: MLE $=1/\\bar X$ but MoM $=\\bar X$","Yes, both equal $1/\\bar X$"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Easy","examShortcut":"Both MLE and MoM for the exponential rate reduce to $\\hat\\theta=1/\\bar X$ - a rare case of exact coincidence for finite $n$.","tipsTricks":["This coincidence is special to the exponential (and a few other one-parameter families) - it does NOT hold in general.","Contrast with $U(0,\\theta)$, where MLE $=X_{(n)}$ but MoM $=2\\bar X$ - clearly different there."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"MLE: maximizing $L(\\theta)=\\theta^ne^{-\\theta\\sum x_i}$ gives $\\hat\\theta_{MLE}=1/\\bar X$."},{"step":2,"text":"MoM: setting $E(X)=1/\\theta=\\bar X$ gives $\\hat\\theta_{MoM}=1/\\bar X$ as well."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-08","section":"Estimation","topic":"Sufficiency and Completeness","subtopic":"Sufficient Estimator and Factorization Theorem","question":"For $X_1,\\dots,X_n$ iid with pdf $f(x,\\theta)=e^{-(x-\\theta)}$ for $x>\\theta$ (shifted exponential), a sufficient statistic for $\\theta$ is","options":["$\\sum X_i^2$","$X_{(1)}$, the minimum order statistic","$X_{(n)}$, the maximum order statistic","$\\bar X$"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Since the support $x>\\theta$ depends on $\\theta$ through the SMALLEST value needed, the binding constraint - and hence the sufficient statistic - is $X_{(1)}$.","tipsTricks":["This mirrors $U(0,\\theta)$ needing $X_{(n)}$: whichever order statistic sits at the boundary that $\\theta$ controls is the sufficient one.","The joint density factors as $e^{n\\theta}e^{-\\sum x_i}\\cdot\\mathbb 1(X_{(1)}>\\theta)$, isolating $X_{(1)}$ as the only $\\theta$-relevant function of the data via the indicator."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$f(x,\\theta)=e^{-(x-\\theta)}\\mathbb 1(x>\\theta)$; the joint density is $e^{n\\theta-\\sum x_i}\\mathbb 1(x_{(1)}>\\theta)$."},{"step":2,"text":"By Fisher-Neyman factorization, this depends on the data (through the indicator) only via $X_{(1)}$, which is therefore sufficient."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-09","section":"Estimation","topic":"Sufficiency and Completeness","subtopic":"Sufficient Estimator and Factorization Theorem","question":"For $X_1,X_2$ iid Poisson$(\\theta)$, why does $T=X_1+2X_2$ fail to be sufficient for $\\theta$, while $T'=X_1+X_2$ succeeds?","options":["The conditional distribution of $(X_1,X_2)$ given $X_1+X_2=t$ is Binomial$(t,\\tfrac12)$, free of $\\theta$; the conditional distribution given $X_1+2X_2$ still involves $\\theta$","$X_1+2X_2$ need not be an integer","$X_1+2X_2$ has larger variance than $X_1+X_2$","Sufficiency requires equal coefficients only for continuous distributions, not discrete ones"],"correctAnswer":0,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Hard","examShortcut":"The joint pmf depends on the data only through $x_1+x_2$ (the exponent of $\\theta$); a differently-weighted combination like $x_1+2x_2$ does not capture this and leaves $\\theta$ in the conditional distribution.","tipsTricks":["Fisher-Neyman factorization is the fast way to see this: $f(x_1,x_2;\\theta)\\propto\\theta^{x_1+x_2}e^{-2\\theta}$ depends on data only via $x_1+x_2$.","A sufficient statistic must match how $\\theta$ actually enters the likelihood - an arbitrary linear combination generally will not."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Joint pmf $\\propto\\theta^{x_1+x_2}e^{-2\\theta}/(x_1!x_2!)$ - the $\\theta$-dependent part involves the data only through $x_1+x_2$."},{"step":2,"text":"Given $T'=x_1+x_2=t$, $X_1\\sim\\text{Binomial}(t,\\tfrac12)$ regardless of $\\theta$ - the conditional law is free of $\\theta$, confirming sufficiency of $T'$."},{"step":3,"text":"No such reduction exists for $T=x_1+2x_2$; its conditional distribution given a fixed value of $T$ still depends on $\\theta$, so $T$ is not sufficient."},{"step":4,"text":"Option (a)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-10","section":"Estimation","topic":"Optimal Estimation","subtopic":"Rao-Blackwell Theorem and UMVUE Construction","question":"If $T$ is sufficient for $\\theta$ and $U$ is any unbiased estimator of $\\theta$, then $\\varphi(T)=E[U\\mid T]$ satisfies","options":["$\\varphi(T)$ always equals $U$","$\\varphi(T)$ is free of $\\theta$, unbiased for $\\theta$, with $\\operatorname{Var}(\\varphi(T))\\le\\operatorname{Var}(U)$ for every $\\theta$","$\\varphi(T)$ may still depend on $\\theta$","$\\varphi(T)$ is unbiased but always has strictly larger variance than $U$"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"This is exactly the Rao-Blackwell theorem: conditioning on a sufficient statistic never increases variance, and sufficiency guarantees the conditional expectation is a genuine (data-only) statistic.","tipsTricks":["Sufficiency of $T$ is what makes $E[U|T]$ computable without knowledge of $\\theta$ - this is the crucial role sufficiency plays here.","Improvement can be strict or the two can coincide (if $U$ was already a function of $T$) - '$\\le$', not '$<$', is the precise claim."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Because $T$ is sufficient, the conditional distribution of the sample given $T$ does not involve $\\theta$, so $\\varphi(T)=E[U|T]$ is a genuine statistic (free of $\\theta$)."},{"step":2,"text":"By the tower property $E[\\varphi(T)]=E[U]=\\theta$ (still unbiased), and by the Rao-Blackwell variance inequality $\\operatorname{Var}(\\varphi(T))\\le\\operatorname{Var}(U)$ for every $\\theta$."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-11","section":"Estimation","topic":"Optimal Estimation","subtopic":"Rao-Blackwell Theorem and UMVUE Construction","question":"Rao-Blackwellizing an unbiased estimator using a statistic $T$ produces the UMVUE if and only if $T$ is","options":["complete only","ancillary","sufficient only","complete and sufficient"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Lehmann-Scheffe: completeness (in addition to sufficiency) is exactly what makes the Rao-Blackwellized estimator unique, and hence the UMVUE.","tipsTricks":["Without completeness, Rao-Blackwellization can still improve variance but need not yield a UNIQUE (and hence optimal) result.","This is the standard two-step recipe examined repeatedly: find a complete sufficient statistic, then Rao-Blackwellize any unbiased estimator using it."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Rao-Blackwell alone (sufficiency) guarantees variance improvement but not uniqueness across different unbiased starting points."},{"step":2,"text":"Lehmann-Scheffe adds completeness of $T$, which forces any two unbiased functions of $T$ to be identical almost surely - giving a unique, hence UMVUE, result."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-12","section":"Estimation","topic":"Optimal Estimation","subtopic":"Cramer-Rao Inequality, MVB and Fisher Information","question":"For $X\\sim\\text{Poisson}(\\theta)$, based on a sample of size $n$, the Cramer-Rao lower bound for the variance of any unbiased estimator of $\\theta$ is","options":["$\\theta^2/n$","$1/(n\\theta^2)$","$n/\\theta$","$\\theta/n$"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Numerical","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Single-observation Fisher information for Poisson is $I(\\theta)=1/\\theta$; for $n$ iid observations, $I_n(\\theta)=n/\\theta$, so CRLB $=\\theta/n$.","tipsTricks":["$\\bar X$ itself attains this bound exactly (its variance is $\\theta/n$), so $\\bar X$ is the UMVUE for Poisson $\\theta$.","Always scale single-observation Fisher information by $n$ before inverting to get the CRLB for a sample."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$\\log f(x,\\theta)=x\\log\\theta-\\theta-\\log x!$; $\\partial^2/\\partial\\theta^2=-x/\\theta^2$; $E[-\\partial^2/\\partial\\theta^2]=E(X)/\\theta^2=1/\\theta$."},{"step":2,"text":"So $I(\\theta)=1/\\theta$ per observation, $I_n(\\theta)=n/\\theta$, and CRLB $=1/I_n(\\theta)=\\theta/n$."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-13","section":"Estimation","topic":"Optimal Estimation","subtopic":"Cramer-Rao Inequality, MVB and Fisher Information","question":"For a single observation from $N(\\mu,\\theta)$ with $\\mu$ known and $\\theta=\\sigma^2$, the Fisher information $I(\\theta)$ equals","options":["$1/\\theta^2$","$1/(2\\theta^2)$","$n/(2\\theta^2)$","$2\\theta^2$"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Numerical","difficulty":"Hard","examShortcut":"$\\log f=-\\tfrac12\\log(2\\pi\\theta)-(x-\\mu)^2/(2\\theta)$; differentiating twice and taking $E[-\\cdot]$ gives $I(\\theta)=1/(2\\theta^2)$ for one observation.","tipsTricks":["For $n$ iid observations, information adds: $I_n(\\theta)=n/(2\\theta^2)$ - watch which version (single-observation or full-sample) a question actually asks for.","This is the same computation underlying the CRLB for estimating a normal variance with known mean."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$\\partial\\log f/\\partial\\theta=-\\tfrac1{2\\theta}+\\tfrac{(x-\\mu)^2}{2\\theta^2}$; $\\partial^2\\log f/\\partial\\theta^2=\\tfrac1{2\\theta^2}-\\tfrac{(x-\\mu)^2}{\\theta^3}$."},{"step":2,"text":"$E[(x-\\mu)^2]=\\theta$, so $E[\\partial^2\\log f/\\partial\\theta^2]=\\tfrac1{2\\theta^2}-\\tfrac1{\\theta^2}=-\\tfrac1{2\\theta^2}$; hence $I(\\theta)=1/(2\\theta^2)$."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-14","section":"Estimation","topic":"Optimal Estimation","subtopic":"Bhattacharya Bounds","question":"The Bhattacharya bound generalizes the Cramer-Rao bound by","options":["applying only to biased estimators","ignoring the likelihood function entirely","incorporating higher-order derivatives of the log-likelihood to give a bound at least as sharp (tight) as the CRLB","requiring the estimator to be sufficient"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Bhattacharya bounds use second- and higher-order score derivatives, giving a sequence of bounds that are never looser than, and often strictly sharper than, the first-order Cramer-Rao bound.","tipsTricks":["When the CRLB is not attained by any estimator, the Bhattacharya bound can still be tight, or closer to the true minimum variance.","The first Bhattacharya bound (using only first derivatives) reduces exactly to the ordinary CRLB - it is a strict generalization."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"CRLB uses only the first derivative of the log-likelihood (the score); Bhattacharya bounds incorporate a vector of higher-order derivatives, producing a bound that dominates (is $\\ge$) the CRLB."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-15","section":"Estimation","topic":"Interval Estimation","subtopic":"Confidence Intervals, Pivots and Optimum Bounds","question":"For $X_1,\\dots,X_n$ iid Exponential with mean $\\theta$, a pivotal quantity for constructing a confidence interval for $\\theta$ is","options":["$\\bar X-\\theta\\sim\\chi^2_1$","$n\\bar X^2/\\theta\\sim F$","$2n\\bar X/\\theta\\sim\\chi^2_{2n}$","$\\bar X/\\theta\\sim N(0,1)$"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Sums of exponential variables relate to the gamma/chi-square family: $2n\\bar X/\\theta\\sim\\chi^2_{2n}$ exactly, for every $n$.","tipsTricks":["This pivot appears repeatedly across confidence-interval and hypothesis-testing questions on the exponential distribution - memorize it.","Being a PIVOT means its distribution is completely free of $\\theta$, which is exactly what makes it usable to build a CI."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"If $X_i$ are iid Exponential(mean $\\theta$), then $2X_i/\\theta\\sim\\chi^2_2$, and by additivity of independent chi-squares, $2\\sum X_i/\\theta=2n\\bar X/\\theta\\sim\\chi^2_{2n}$."},{"step":2,"text":"This quantity's distribution does not depend on $\\theta$, so it is a valid pivot for a CI."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-16","section":"Estimation","topic":"Interval Estimation","subtopic":"Confidence Intervals, Pivots and Optimum Bounds","question":"A 95% confidence interval for $\\mu$ (with $\\sigma=4$ known), based on a sample of size $n$, is reported as $(18.04,\\ 21.96)$. The sample mean and sample size are","options":["$\\bar X=20,\\ n=16$","$\\bar X=20,\\ n=64$","$\\bar X=20,\\ n=4$","$\\bar X=19,\\ n=16$"],"correctAnswer":0,"questionType":"Numerical","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Midpoint $=20=\\bar X$; half-width $=1.96=1.96(4)/\\sqrt n\\Rightarrow\\sqrt n=4\\Rightarrow n=16$.","tipsTricks":["The midpoint of any symmetric CI is always the point estimate; only the half-width carries information about $n$.","Solve $1.96\\sigma/\\sqrt n=\\text{half-width}$ for $n$ - a frequent 'reverse-engineer the CI' question type."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Midpoint: $(18.04+21.96)/2=20=\\bar X$."},{"step":2,"text":"Half-width: $(21.96-18.04)/2=1.96$; setting $1.96=1.96\\times4/\\sqrt n$ gives $\\sqrt n=4\\Rightarrow n=16$."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (a)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-17","section":"Estimation","topic":"Interval Estimation","subtopic":"Resampling: Bootstrap and Jackknife","question":"The bootstrap method estimates the sampling distribution of a statistic by","options":["assuming normality and computing exact moments","requiring a known parametric family for the population","resampling with replacement from the observed data","resampling without replacement, taking exactly half the data each time"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Easy","examShortcut":"Bootstrap = resample WITH replacement from the empirical distribution (the observed sample) to approximate the true sampling distribution non-parametrically.","tipsTricks":["'With replacement' is essential - it is what allows bootstrap samples to differ from each other and from the original sample.","The bootstrap requires no assumption about the population's parametric family, unlike classical CI theory."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Bootstrap draws many resamples of size $n$, WITH replacement, from the original data, computes the statistic on each, and uses the resulting empirical distribution to approximate the true sampling distribution."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-18","section":"Estimation","topic":"Interval Estimation","subtopic":"Resampling: Bootstrap and Jackknife","question":"The jackknife estimator of the bias of a statistic $T_n$ is based on","options":["bootstrap resampling with replacement","leave-one-out recomputation of the statistic","a single random split of the data into two halves","repeating the full-sample estimate $n$ times"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Jackknife systematically deletes one observation at a time, recomputes $T_{(i)}$ each time, and uses the spread of these leave-one-out values to estimate bias (and variance).","tipsTricks":["Jackknife bias estimate: $(n-1)(\\bar T_{(\\cdot)}-T_n)$, where $\\bar T_{(\\cdot)}$ is the average of the $n$ leave-one-out estimates.","Unlike bootstrap, jackknife is deterministic (exactly $n$ leave-one-out samples, no randomness)."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"For $i=1,\\dots,n$, compute $T_{(i)}$ from the sample with observation $i$ removed; these $n$ 'leave-one-out' values are the basis of the jackknife bias and variance estimators."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-19","section":"Estimation","topic":"Sufficiency and Completeness","subtopic":"Completeness, Minimal Sufficiency, Ancillarity, Basu's Theorem","question":"For $X\\sim N(0,\\theta)$ (mean known $=0$, $\\theta$ the unknown variance), which statement is correct regarding completeness?","options":["$X$ is complete for $\\theta$, but $X^2$ is not","$X$ alone is not complete for $\\theta$; but $X^2$ is complete for $\\theta$","Neither $X$ nor $X^2$ is complete for $\\theta$","Both $X$ and $X^2$ are complete for $\\theta$"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Hard","examShortcut":"$g(X)=X$ has $E_\\theta[X]=0$ for every $\\theta$ without $X\\equiv0$ a.s., so $X$ is not complete; but $X^2/\\theta\\sim\\chi^2_1$ scaling makes $X^2$ complete.","tipsTricks":["A single counterexample function with zero expectation for all $\\theta$ (here, $g(X)=X$ itself) is enough to break completeness.","This exact pair ($X$ vs $X^2$ for $N(0,\\theta)$) is a favourite conceptual contrast in this topic."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$E_\\theta(X)=0$ for every $\\theta$, yet $X$ is not a.s. $0$ - this single example already shows $X$ is NOT complete for $\\theta$."},{"step":2,"text":"By contrast, $X^2$ is a one-to-one function of a scaled chi-square variable and can be shown to be complete sufficient for $\\theta$ in this family."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-20","section":"Estimation","topic":"Sufficiency and Completeness","subtopic":"Completeness, Minimal Sufficiency, Ancillarity, Basu's Theorem","question":"For iid observations from $U(\\theta-\\tfrac12,\\theta+\\tfrac12)$, the range $R=X_{(n)}-X_{(1)}$ is","options":["ancillary - its distribution does not depend on $\\theta$ at all","distributed as Poisson$(\\theta)$","a complete sufficient statistic for $\\theta$","an unbiased estimator of $\\theta$"],"correctAnswer":0,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Hard","examShortcut":"Shifting $\\theta$ shifts the whole sample by a constant, leaving the range unchanged in distribution - the textbook example of an ancillary statistic.","tipsTricks":["$(X_{(1)},X_{(n)})$ jointly is minimal sufficient but NOT complete, precisely because a function of it (the range) is ancillary - a nonzero function with $\\theta$-free expectation.","This links directly to why the MLE is non-unique for this family: the location is only pinned down up to this ancillary range."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Because the family is a location family (shifted by $\\theta$), the DIFFERENCE between any two order statistics has a distribution free of $\\theta$ - hence $R$ is ancillary."},{"step":2,"text":"This also shows $(X_{(1)},X_{(n)})$, while minimal sufficient, cannot be complete: $R$ (a nonzero function of it) has $\\theta$-free expectation."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (a)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-21","section":"Estimation","topic":"Point Estimation","subtopic":"Characteristics of a Good Estimator (Unbiasedness, Consistency, Efficiency, MSE)","question":"For iid observations from a standard Cauchy distribution, the sample mean $\\bar X$ as an estimator of the location parameter","options":["is inconsistent - $\\bar X$ has exactly the same Cauchy distribution regardless of $n$","equals the maximum likelihood estimator","is unbiased with variance decreasing as $1/n$","is consistent and efficient"],"correctAnswer":0,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"The Cauchy distribution has no finite mean or variance; remarkably, $\\bar X$ for $n$ iid standard Cauchy variables has EXACTLY the same standard Cauchy distribution as a single observation - it never concentrates.","tipsTricks":["The sample MEDIAN, by contrast, IS a consistent estimator of the Cauchy location parameter - medians are far more robust than means for heavy-tailed distributions.","This is the standard counterexample showing 'more data always helps' is false without finite-variance assumptions."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"For iid standard Cauchy $X_1,\\dots,X_n$, the characteristic function of $\\bar X$ equals that of a single $X_i$ - so $\\bar X\\stackrel{d}{=}X_1$ for every $n$, meaning $\\bar X$ does not concentrate around the true location as $n\\to\\infty$."},{"step":2,"text":"Hence $\\bar X$ is inconsistent for the Cauchy location; the sample median is the standard robust, consistent alternative here."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (a)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-22","section":"Estimation","topic":"Sufficiency and Completeness","subtopic":"Sufficient Estimator and Factorization Theorem","question":"For $X_1,\\dots,X_n$ iid $N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ with both parameters unknown, a complete sufficient statistic for $(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ is","options":["$\\bar X$ alone","(sample median, sample range) jointly","$(\\bar X,\\ S^2)$ jointly","$S^2$ alone"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"The two-parameter normal family has $(\\bar X,S^2)$ (equivalently $(\\sum X_i,\\sum X_i^2)$) as its complete sufficient statistic - neither component alone suffices for both parameters.","tipsTricks":["Neither $\\bar X$ alone nor $S^2$ alone is sufficient for the FULL pair $(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ - sufficiency here is inherently two-dimensional.","By Basu's theorem, $\\bar X$ and $S^2$ are also INDEPENDENT for the normal family - a separate, equally famous fact."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"The joint density's exponential-family form shows the natural sufficient statistic is $(\\sum x_i,\\sum x_i^2)$, a one-to-one function of $(\\bar X,S^2)$; completeness of the two-parameter exponential family carries over to this pair."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-23","section":"Estimation","topic":"Optimal Estimation","subtopic":"Rao-Blackwell Theorem and UMVUE Construction","question":"For $X_1,\\dots,X_n$ iid Poisson$(\\theta)$, with $T=\\sum X_i$ complete sufficient, the UMVUE of $\\theta^2$ is","options":["$T(T-1)/n^2$","$T^2/n^2$","$T(T+1)/n^2$","$T(T-1)/n$"],"correctAnswer":0,"questionType":"Numerical","difficulty":"Hard","examShortcut":"Using the Poisson factorial-moment identity $E[T(T-1)]=(n\\theta)^2$, dividing by $n^2$ gives an exactly unbiased estimator of $\\theta^2$ built from the complete sufficient $T$.","tipsTricks":["$T^2/n^2$ is NOT unbiased for $\\theta^2$ (it has an extra $+\\theta/n$ bias term) - the falling-factorial correction $T(T-1)$ is what removes this bias exactly.","This falling-factorial trick generalizes: the UMVUE of $\\theta^k$ is $T(T-1)\\cdots(T-k+1)/n^k$."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Since $T\\sim\\text{Poisson}(n\\theta)$, its factorial moments satisfy $E[T(T-1)]=(n\\theta)^2$, so $E[T(T-1)/n^2]=\\theta^2$ exactly."},{"step":2,"text":"By Lehmann-Scheffe (T is complete sufficient), this unbiased function of $T$ is the UMVUE of $\\theta^2$."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (a)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-24","section":"Estimation","topic":"Interval Estimation","subtopic":"Confidence Intervals, Pivots and Optimum Bounds","question":"To halve the width of a 95% confidence interval for $\\mu$ (with $\\sigma$ known), the sample size must be","options":["doubled","increased by 50%","quadrupled","halved"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Easy","examShortcut":"CI width $\\propto1/\\sqrt n$; halving the width requires $\\sqrt n$ to double, i.e. $n$ to quadruple.","tipsTricks":["This inverse-square-root relationship between precision and sample size is worth memorizing as a quick mental-math check.","The same logic applies to standard errors generally: to halve any SE that scales as $1/\\sqrt n$, you must quadruple $n$."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Width $\\propto\\sigma/\\sqrt n$. Setting new width $=\\tfrac12\\times$ old width requires $\\sqrt{n_{\\text{new}}}=2\\sqrt{n_{\\text{old}}}$, i.e. $n_{\\text{new}}=4n_{\\text{old}}$."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-EST-25","section":"Estimation","topic":"Methods of Estimation","subtopic":"Maximum Likelihood Estimation","question":"If $\\hat\\theta$ is the maximum likelihood estimator of $\\theta$, and $g$ is any function, the MLE of $g(\\theta)$ is","options":["$g^{-1}(\\hat\\theta)$","equal to $\\hat\\theta$ only if $g$ is the identity function","undefined unless $g$ is linear","$g(\\hat\\theta)$ - the invariance property of MLEs"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"The MLE invariance property: for ANY function $g$ (not just one-to-one ones, via the induced-likelihood extension), the MLE of $g(\\theta)$ is simply $g(\\hat\\theta)$.","tipsTricks":["This is why, e.g., the MLE of $\\theta^2$ or $e^{-\\theta}$ can be written down immediately once the MLE of $\\theta$ is known - no fresh derivation needed.","Invariance is a property MLEs enjoy that many other estimators (like unbiased or MoM estimators) do NOT - e.g. $g(\\hat\\theta_{MoM})$ need not be the MoM estimator of $g(\\theta)$."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"By the invariance principle of maximum likelihood, if $\\hat\\theta$ maximizes the likelihood for $\\theta$, then $g(\\hat\\theta)$ maximizes the induced likelihood for $g(\\theta)$, for any function $g$."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-01","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Hypothesis Testing","subtopic":"Simple and Composite Hypotheses, Two Kinds of Error, Size and Power","question":"The power of a test at an alternative $\\theta\\in H_1$ is defined as","options":["$\\beta(\\theta)=P_\\theta(\\text{accept }H_0)$","$\\alpha=P(\\text{reject }H_0\\mid H_0\\text{ true})$","$1-\\beta(\\theta)=P_\\theta(\\text{reject }H_0)$","$1-\\alpha$"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Easy","examShortcut":"Power is the probability of correctly rejecting $H_0$ when the alternative is true - i.e. $1$ minus the Type II error probability at that alternative.","tipsTricks":["Power is a FUNCTION of $\\theta\\in H_1$, not a single number, unless the alternative is simple.","Do not confuse power ($1-\\beta$) with $1-\\alpha$, which is the probability of correctly ACCEPTING $H_0$ when it is true."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"By definition, power at $\\theta$ is $P_\\theta(\\text{reject }H_0)=1-P_\\theta(\\text{accept }H_0)=1-\\beta(\\theta)$."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-02","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Simple and Composite Hypotheses, Two Kinds of Error, Size and Power","subtopic":"Simple and Composite Hypotheses, Two Kinds of Error, Size and Power","question":"For a composite null hypothesis $H_0$, the size of a test with critical region $C$ is defined as","options":["$\\sup_{\\theta\\in H_0}P_\\theta(C)$","the average of $P_\\theta(C)$ over $H_0$","the Type II error probability","$\\inf_{\\theta\\in H_0}P_\\theta(C)$"],"correctAnswer":0,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"For a composite null, size is the SUPREMUM (worst case) of the rejection probability over all $\\theta\\in H_0$ - guaranteeing the Type I error never exceeds this bound anywhere in $H_0$.","tipsTricks":["For a SIMPLE null ($H_0:\\theta=\\theta_0$), size just reduces to $P_{\\theta_0}(C)$ - the supremum definition is only needed for composite nulls.","Using $\\inf$ instead of $\\sup$ would understate the true worst-case Type I error rate - a common conceptual trap."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"To control the Type I error rate uniformly across a composite null, size must be defined as the WORST (largest) rejection probability attainable within $H_0$, i.e. $\\sup_{\\theta\\in H_0}P_\\theta(C)$."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (a)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-03","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Hypothesis Testing","subtopic":"Neyman-Pearson Lemma and Best Critical Region","question":"$X$ is a single observation from Exponential$(\\theta)$ with pdf $f(x,\\theta)=\\theta e^{-\\theta x}$, $x>0$. To test $H_0:\\theta=1$ against $H_1:\\theta=2$, the test rejects $H_0$ if $X<0.5$. The size and power of this test are respectively","options":["$0.50,\\ 0.50$","$0.63,\\ 0.39$","$0.39,\\ 0.39$","$0.39,\\ 0.63$"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Numerical","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Size $=P(X<0.5\\mid\\theta=1)=1-e^{-0.5}\\approx0.39$; power $=P(X<0.5\\mid\\theta=2)=1-e^{-1}\\approx0.63$.","tipsTricks":["For Exponential$(\\theta)$, $P(X<c)=1-e^{-\\theta c}$ - substitute $\\theta$ and $c$ directly, no calculus needed.","Since $H_1$ has a larger rate (smaller mean), small $X$ correctly favours $H_1$ - the direction of the critical region matters as much as its size."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Size: $P(X<0.5\\mid\\theta=1)=1-e^{-1(0.5)}=1-e^{-0.5}=1-0.6065=0.3935\\approx0.39$."},{"step":2,"text":"Power: $P(X<0.5\\mid\\theta=2)=1-e^{-2(0.5)}=1-e^{-1}=1-0.3679=0.6321\\approx0.63$."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-04","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Hypothesis Testing","subtopic":"Neyman-Pearson Lemma and Best Critical Region","question":"For $X\\sim\\text{Poisson}(\\theta)$, testing $H_0:\\theta=\\theta_0$ against $H_1:\\theta=\\theta_1$ with $\\theta_1>\\theta_0$, the Neyman-Pearson best critical region is of the form","options":["$X\\le c$","$X=c$ exactly","$|X-\\theta_0|\\ge c$","$X\\ge c$"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Likelihood ratio $f(x;\\theta_0)/f(x;\\theta_1)=e^{\\theta_1-\\theta_0}(\\theta_0/\\theta_1)^x$ is decreasing in $x$ since $\\theta_0/\\theta_1<1$; small ratio (reject $H_0$) means large $x$.","tipsTricks":["$\\theta_1>\\theta_0$ means $H_1$ predicts a LARGER mean, so it makes sense that large observed values favour $H_1$ - a useful sanity check on the direction.","Always derive the direction from the monotonicity of the likelihood ratio - never guess it from intuition alone, especially for asymmetric families."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"The NP lemma rejects $H_0$ where the likelihood ratio $f(x;\\theta_0)/f(x;\\theta_1)$ is small; since this ratio is a decreasing function of $x$ here, 'small ratio' corresponds to 'large $x$'."},{"step":2,"text":"So the best critical region is $X\\ge c$ for a suitable threshold $c$."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-05","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Hypothesis Testing","subtopic":"UMP, MLR, Unbiased and Similar Tests","question":"A family with Monotone Likelihood Ratio (MLR) in a statistic $T$ guarantees the existence of a UMP test for","options":["two-sided hypotheses, $H_0:\\theta=\\theta_0$ vs $H_1:\\theta\\ne\\theta_0$","only point-null versus point-alternative tests","one-sided hypotheses, e.g. $H_0:\\theta\\le\\theta_0$ vs $H_1:\\theta>\\theta_0$","any hypothesis, one- or two-sided, without exception"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"MLR guarantees a UMP test exists for ONE-SIDED composite alternatives; for two-sided alternatives no UMP test generally exists, and one instead seeks a UMP-unbiased (UMPU) test.","tipsTricks":["This is why textbooks pair 'MLR family' with 'one-sided UMP test' as a fixed combination - watch for two-sided alternatives disguised as a trick option.","Common MLR families: one-parameter exponential families (Binomial, Poisson, Normal with known variance, Exponential) all have MLR in their natural sufficient statistic."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"The Karlin-Rubin theorem states that MLR in $T$ ensures a UMP level-$\\alpha$ test exists for one-sided hypotheses, of the form 'reject if $T$ is large' (or small, depending on direction)."},{"step":2,"text":"For two-sided alternatives, no test can be uniformly most powerful against alternatives on both sides simultaneously."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-06","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"UMP, MLR, Unbiased and Similar Tests","subtopic":"UMP, MLR, Unbiased and Similar Tests","question":"For testing $H_0:\\theta=\\theta_0$ vs $H_1:\\theta\\ne\\theta_0$ in a one-parameter exponential family, since no UMP test exists in general, one instead seeks a","options":["test with power exactly $0.5$ everywhere","UMP Unbiased (UMPU) test","test most powerful against a single chosen alternative only","randomized test only"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"The standard resolution to 'no UMP test exists for two-sided alternatives' is to restrict attention to UNBIASED tests and find the best one within that class - the UMPU test.","tipsTricks":["UMPU tests for two-sided normal-mean and normal-variance problems are the classical, most commonly examined examples.","Unbiasedness here means power $\\ge\\alpha$ at every alternative and $\\le\\alpha$ throughout $H_0$ - it rules out perverse tests that are powerful in only one direction."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Restricting the search to unbiased tests (power never below the size, everywhere in $H_1$) makes the two-sided testing problem tractable, yielding a UMP-unbiased (UMPU) test as the standard solution."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-07","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Hypothesis Testing","subtopic":"Randomized Tests","question":"$X\\sim\\text{Binomial}(10,\\,p)$. To test $H_0:p=0.5$ vs $H_1:p>0.5$ at nominal level $\\alpha=0.05$ using the NON-randomized test 'reject $H_0$ if $X\\ge9$', the EXACT size of this test is","options":["$0.0547$","$0.1074$","$0.0107$","$0.05$ exactly"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Numerical","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"$P(X\\ge9\\mid p=0.5)=\\dfrac{\\binom{10}{9}+\\binom{10}{10}}{2^{10}}=\\dfrac{11}{1024}\\approx0.0107$ - discreteness prevents hitting $\\alpha=0.05$ exactly without randomizing.","tipsTricks":["Discreteness of the Binomial means a non-randomized test can rarely achieve a nominal $\\alpha$ exactly - only a randomized test at the boundary point ($X=8$ here) can hit $0.05$ precisely.","$P(X\\ge8\\mid p=0.5)=56/1024\\approx0.0547$ overshoots $0.05$, while $P(X\\ge9)\\approx0.0107$ undershoots it - the true size always lies strictly below the nominal level for the conservative non-randomized choice."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$P(X\\ge9)=P(X=9)+P(X=10)=\\dfrac{\\binom{10}{9}+\\binom{10}{10}}{2^{10}}=\\dfrac{10+1}{1024}=\\dfrac{11}{1024}=0.01074$."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-08","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Hypothesis Testing","subtopic":"Randomized Tests","question":"$X\\sim\\text{Poisson}(\\lambda)$. To test $H_0:\\lambda=2$ vs $H_1:\\lambda=3$, the randomized test $\\varphi(x)=1$ if $x\\ge4$; $\\varphi(x)=0.4$ if $x=3$; $\\varphi(x)=0$ if $x\\le2$ has size and power respectively approximately","options":["$0.442,\\ 0.215$","$0.215,\\ 0.442$","$0.323,\\ 0.577$","$0.180,\\ 0.224$"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Numerical","difficulty":"Hard","examShortcut":"Size $=P(X\\ge4\\mid\\lambda=2)+0.4P(X=3\\mid\\lambda=2)$; power is the same formula evaluated at $\\lambda=3$.","tipsTricks":["Compute $P(X\\ge4)=1-P(X\\le3)$ from the Poisson pmf, then add the randomization term $0.4\\times P(X=3)$ separately - do not fold the randomized point into the 'always reject' region.","Verify power exceeds size (as it must for any sensible test with $\\lambda_1>\\lambda_0$) as a quick sanity check."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"At $\\lambda=2$: $P(X\\le3)=e^{-2}(1+2+2+\\tfrac43)=0.8571$, so $P(X\\ge4)=0.1429$; $P(X=3)=\\tfrac{8}{6}e^{-2}=0.1804$."},{"step":2,"text":"Size $=0.1429+0.4(0.1804)=0.1429+0.0722=0.2151\\approx0.215$."},{"step":3,"text":"At $\\lambda=3$: $P(X\\le3)=e^{-3}(1+3+4.5+4.5)=0.6472$, so $P(X\\ge4)=0.3528$; $P(X=3)=4.5e^{-3}=0.2240$."},{"step":4,"text":"Power $=0.3528+0.4(0.2240)=0.3528+0.0896=0.4424\\approx0.442$."},{"step":5,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-09","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Hypothesis Testing","subtopic":"Randomized Tests","question":"Randomized tests are needed in hypothesis testing primarily because","options":["continuous distributions require randomization to define a critical region","for discrete distributions, no non-randomized critical region can generally achieve an arbitrary prescribed size $\\alpha$ exactly","they always have strictly higher power than non-randomized tests","randomization removes the need to specify $H_1$"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Discreteness of the sample space (Binomial, Poisson, etc.) means the achievable exact sizes form a finite set of values; randomizing at one boundary point lets us hit any $\\alpha$ in between exactly.","tipsTricks":["For CONTINUOUS distributions, a non-randomized test can typically achieve any $\\alpha$ exactly - randomization is a discreteness-specific device.","Randomized tests do not improve power in any absolute sense; they trade off precisely hitting a target size against a small amount of extra 'coin-flip' variability."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"With a discrete test statistic, only finitely many rejection-region sizes are achievable exactly; to hit an arbitrary $\\alpha$ strictly between two such achievable sizes, one randomizes (e.g. via a coin toss) at the boundary observation."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-10","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Sequential and Decision Theory","subtopic":"Wald's SPRT, OC and ASN Functions","question":"In Wald's SPRT for testing $H_0$ vs $H_1$ with Type I error $\\alpha$ and Type II error $\\beta$, the approximate stopping bounds $A$ (lower) and $B$ (upper) for the likelihood ratio satisfy","options":["$A=\\alpha$, $B=\\beta$","$A\\approx\\dfrac{\\beta}{1-\\alpha}$, $B\\approx\\dfrac{1-\\beta}{\\alpha}$","$A\\approx\\dfrac{\\alpha}{1-\\beta}$, $B\\approx\\dfrac{1-\\alpha}{\\beta}$","$A\\approx\\dfrac{1-\\beta}{\\alpha}$, $B\\approx\\dfrac{\\beta}{1-\\alpha}$"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Wald's bounds: continue sampling while $A<\\text{LR}<B$, with $A\\approx\\beta/(1-\\alpha)$ (lower) and $B\\approx(1-\\beta)/\\alpha$ (upper) - getting these two the wrong way round is the classic slip.","tipsTricks":["A quick sanity check: for small $\\alpha,\\beta$, $B$ is a large number ($\\approx1/\\alpha$) and $A$ is a small number ($\\approx\\beta$) - if your formula gives it backwards, you have swapped them.","These approximate bounds are derived by ignoring the 'overshoot' at the boundary when the SPRT actually stops - an approximation, not exact."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Wald's approximation sets $A\\approx\\dfrac{P(\\text{reject }H_0\\mid H_1)}{P(\\text{reject }H_0\\mid H_0)}=\\dfrac{1-\\beta}{\\alpha}$... more precisely the SPRT continues while $\\dfrac{\\beta}{1-\\alpha}<\\text{LR}_{10}<\\dfrac{1-\\beta}{\\alpha}$, i.e. $A=\\dfrac{\\beta}{1-\\alpha}$ and $B=\\dfrac{1-\\beta}{\\alpha}$."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-11","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Sequential and Decision Theory","subtopic":"Wald's SPRT, OC and ASN Functions","question":"In SPRT, the Operating Characteristic function $L(\\theta)$ gives the probability of accepting $H_0$. At $\\theta=\\theta_0$ (i.e. under $H_0$), $L(\\theta_0)$ approximately equals","options":["$1-\\alpha$","$1-\\beta$","$\\alpha$","$\\beta$"],"correctAnswer":0,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"$L(\\theta_0)$ is the probability of (correctly) accepting $H_0$ when $H_0$ is true, which is $1$ minus the Type I error rate, i.e. $1-\\alpha$.","tipsTricks":["Similarly, $L(\\theta_1)\\approx\\beta$ - the probability of (incorrectly) accepting $H_0$ when $H_1$ is true.","The OC curve is the SPRT analogue of the power function, but expressed as the probability of ACCEPTING (not rejecting) $H_0$."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$L(\\theta)=P_\\theta(\\text{accept }H_0)$; at $\\theta=\\theta_0$, accepting $H_0$ is the correct decision, so $L(\\theta_0)=1-P_{\\theta_0}(\\text{reject }H_0)=1-\\alpha$."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (a)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-12","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Sequential and Decision Theory","subtopic":"Wald's SPRT, OC and ASN Functions","question":"The Average Sample Number (ASN) of Wald's SPRT, compared to a fixed-sample-size Neyman-Pearson test achieving the SAME $\\alpha,\\beta$, is generally","options":["always larger than the fixed-sample test","smaller - SPRT is more efficient on average, often needing roughly half the expected sample size","exactly equal to the fixed sample size","infinite"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"SPRT's key advantage (Wald-Wolfowitz optimality) is achieving the same $(\\alpha,\\beta)$ error rates with a SMALLER expected sample size than any fixed-sample test.","tipsTricks":["This efficiency gain is largest when the true parameter is close to $\\theta_0$ or $\\theta_1$ (the hypothesized values); near the 'indifference zone' midpoint, ASN can be larger.","SPRT trades a fixed sample size for a RANDOM (but typically smaller on average) one - this is its central practical appeal in sequential analysis."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"The Wald-Wolfowitz theorem proves that among all tests (sequential or fixed) achieving given $\\alpha,\\beta$, Wald's SPRT minimizes the expected sample size at $\\theta=\\theta_0$ and $\\theta=\\theta_1$ simultaneously."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-13","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Hypothesis Testing","subtopic":"Likelihood Ratio Test","question":"The likelihood ratio test statistic for testing $H_0:\\theta\\in\\Theta_0$ against the general alternative $\\theta\\in\\Theta$ is defined as","options":["$\\lambda=\\dfrac{\\sup_{\\theta\\in\\Theta_0}L(\\theta)}{\\sup_{\\theta\\in\\Theta}L(\\theta)}$, and $H_0$ is rejected for small $\\lambda$","$\\lambda$ is always $\\ge1$","$\\lambda=\\dfrac{\\sup_{\\theta\\in\\Theta}L(\\theta)}{\\sup_{\\theta\\in\\Theta_0}L(\\theta)}$, and $H_0$ is rejected for small $\\lambda$","$\\lambda=L(\\theta_0)$ alone, ignoring the alternative"],"correctAnswer":0,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"LRT statistic $\\lambda\\in(0,1]$ compares the best-fit likelihood restricted to $H_0$ against the best-fit likelihood over the whole parameter space; small $\\lambda$ signals the data fit $H_0$ poorly relative to the unrestricted fit.","tipsTricks":["$\\lambda$ always lies in $(0,1]$ since $\\Theta_0\\subseteq\\Theta$ makes the numerator's supremum never exceed the denominator's.","Rejecting for SMALL $\\lambda$ (not large) is the correct direction - a frequent sign error."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"By construction $\\lambda=\\sup_{\\Theta_0}L/\\sup_\\Theta L\\le1$; values close to $1$ mean $H_0$ fits nearly as well as the unrestricted model, while small $\\lambda$ indicates a poor fit under $H_0$ - hence 'reject for small $\\lambda$'."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (a)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-14","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Hypothesis Testing","subtopic":"Likelihood Ratio Test","question":"Under $H_0$, as $n\\to\\infty$, $-2\\log\\lambda$ (where $\\lambda$ is the LRT statistic) asymptotically follows","options":["$t_{n-1}$","$N(0,1)$","an $F$ distribution","$\\chi^2_r$, where $r$ is the number of restrictions imposed by $H_0$"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Wilks' theorem: $-2\\log\\lambda\\xrightarrow{d}\\chi^2_r$ under $H_0$, with $r=\\dim(\\Theta)-\\dim(\\Theta_0)$, the number of independent restrictions $H_0$ imposes.","tipsTricks":["This asymptotic result underlies most 'large-sample LRT' procedures used when an exact finite-sample distribution of $\\lambda$ is unavailable.","$r$ counts PARAMETER RESTRICTIONS, not sample size or number of observations - a common point of confusion."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Wilks' theorem states that, under standard regularity conditions and $H_0$ true, $-2\\log\\lambda$ converges in distribution to $\\chi^2_r$, where $r$ is the difference in dimension between $\\Theta$ and $\\Theta_0$."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-15","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Sequential and Decision Theory","subtopic":"Decision Theory, Loss, Risk and Bayes Estimators","question":"In decision-theoretic estimation, the risk function $R(\\theta,\\delta)$ of a decision rule $\\delta$ is defined as","options":["the maximum possible loss over all $\\theta$","$E_\\theta[L(\\theta,\\delta(X))]$","$\\operatorname{Var}_\\theta[\\delta(X)]$","$L(\\theta,\\delta(X))$ for a single realization of $X$"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Easy","examShortcut":"Risk is the EXPECTED loss, averaged over the sampling distribution of $X$ at each fixed $\\theta$ - not the loss for one particular realization.","tipsTricks":["Under squared error loss $L(\\theta,\\delta)=(\\theta-\\delta)^2$, risk reduces exactly to MSE: $R(\\theta,\\delta)=E_\\theta[(\\delta(X)-\\theta)^2]$.","Risk is a FUNCTION of $\\theta$ (a curve), which is why comparing decision rules typically means comparing their entire risk functions, not a single number."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"By definition, $R(\\theta,\\delta)=E_\\theta[L(\\theta,\\delta(X))]$, the expectation of the loss taken over the randomness in $X$ for each fixed $\\theta$."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-16","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Sequential and Decision Theory","subtopic":"Decision Theory, Loss, Risk and Bayes Estimators","question":"A decision rule that minimizes the MAXIMUM risk over all $\\theta$ is called","options":["admissible","UMVUE","Bayes","minimax"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Easy","examShortcut":"'Minimax' literally means minimizing the maximum (worst-case) risk across the parameter space - a conservative, distribution-free criterion.","tipsTricks":["Contrast with a BAYES rule, which minimizes the AVERAGE risk with respect to a prior distribution on $\\theta$, not the worst case.","Minimax rules are often (though not always) also Bayes rules with respect to a specific 'least favourable' prior."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"By definition, a minimax rule $\\delta^*$ satisfies $\\sup_\\theta R(\\theta,\\delta^*)=\\inf_\\delta\\sup_\\theta R(\\theta,\\delta)$ - it minimizes the worst-case risk."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-17","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Sequential and Decision Theory","subtopic":"Decision Theory, Loss, Risk and Bayes Estimators","question":"A decision rule $\\delta$ is INADMISSIBLE if","options":["it has the single highest risk at exactly one value of $\\theta$","it is unbiased","there exists $\\delta'$ with $R(\\theta,\\delta')\\le R(\\theta,\\delta)$ for every $\\theta$, and strictly less for at least one $\\theta$","it is a randomized rule"],"correctAnswer":2,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Inadmissibility means another rule DOMINATES it: never worse anywhere, and strictly better somewhere - so there is no reason to ever use the dominated rule.","tipsTricks":["Admissibility is a MINIMAL requirement (not being dominated), not a claim of overall optimality - an admissible rule can still have poor risk everywhere.","Both minimax and Bayes rules are typically (though with some technical exceptions) admissible."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$\\delta$ is inadmissible if some other rule $\\delta'$ is at least as good at every $\\theta$ and strictly better at some $\\theta$ - i.e. $\\delta'$ dominates $\\delta$, making $\\delta$ pointless to use."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (c)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-18","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Sequential and Decision Theory","subtopic":"Decision Theory, Loss, Risk and Bayes Estimators","question":"Under squared error loss, the Bayes estimator of $\\theta$ given data $x$ is","options":["the maximum likelihood estimate","the prior mean, ignoring the data","the posterior mode","the posterior mean $E[\\theta\\mid x]$"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"For squared error loss specifically, the Bayes estimator (minimizer of posterior expected loss) is exactly the posterior mean - a standard, frequently-tested result.","tipsTricks":["Under ABSOLUTE error loss, the Bayes estimator would instead be the posterior MEDIAN - the optimal point estimate depends on the loss function chosen.","Under 0-1 loss (for discrete $\\theta$), the Bayes estimator is the posterior MODE (MAP estimate) - three different loss functions, three different Bayes rules."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Minimizing $E[(\\theta-a)^2\\mid x]$ over $a$ gives $a=E[\\theta\\mid x]$, the posterior mean - a standard calculus result (complete the square, or differentiate and set to zero)."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-19","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Hypothesis Testing","subtopic":"UMP, MLR, Unbiased and Similar Tests","question":"A critical region $C$ is called 'similar' with respect to a nuisance parameter if","options":["it has size exactly $0.5$","its size is the same constant for every value of the nuisance parameter, throughout $H_0$","it is necessarily randomized","it contains only sample points that resemble each other"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Hard","examShortcut":"A similar region has rejection probability that does NOT vary with the nuisance parameter anywhere in $H_0$ - essential when $H_0$ is composite due to an unknown nuisance parameter.","tipsTricks":["Similar regions are the standard tool for handling composite nulls with nuisance parameters, often constructed via conditioning on a sufficient statistic for the nuisance parameter.","Every similar test is, by construction, also of constant size across the nuisance parameter's range - the two ideas are directly linked."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"When $H_0$ involves a nuisance parameter $\\eta$ (e.g. $H_0:\\mu=\\mu_0$, $\\sigma$ unknown), a region $C$ is 'similar on the boundary' if $P_{\\theta_0,\\eta}(C)=\\alpha$ for every value of $\\eta$ - i.e. its size is unaffected by the nuisance parameter."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-20","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Hypothesis Testing","subtopic":"UMP, MLR, Unbiased and Similar Tests","question":"A test $\\varphi$ is said to be UNBIASED for testing $H_0$ against $H_1$ if","options":["it has power $1$ at every alternative","its power at every alternative in $H_1$ is at least as large as its size","its power equals exactly $\\alpha$ everywhere","it never commits a Type II error"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"Unbiasedness of a test requires the power function to be $\\ge\\alpha$ throughout $H_1$ and $\\le\\alpha$ throughout $H_0$ - the test should never be 'more likely' to reject under a false alternative than under some point of $H_0$ itself.","tipsTricks":["An unbiased test rules out perverse behaviour where power at some alternative dips BELOW the size - which would mean the test is less likely to detect that alternative than it is to make a Type I error.","UMPU tests are found by first restricting to the (typically large) class of unbiased tests, then finding the most powerful one within that class."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Formally, $\\varphi$ is unbiased if $\\beta_\\varphi(\\theta)\\ge\\alpha$ for all $\\theta\\in H_1$ and $\\beta_\\varphi(\\theta)\\le\\alpha$ for all $\\theta\\in H_0$, where $\\beta_\\varphi$ is the power function."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-21","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Hypothesis Testing","subtopic":"Simple and Composite Hypotheses, Two Kinds of Error, Size and Power","question":"The power function of a test $\\varphi$, $\\beta(\\theta)=E_\\theta[\\varphi(X)]$, is defined","options":["as the constant $1-\\alpha$ everywhere","only at a single point, $\\theta=\\theta_0$","only for $\\theta\\in H_1$","for every $\\theta$ in the full parameter space, both under $H_0$ and $H_1$"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Definition","difficulty":"Easy","examShortcut":"The power function $\\beta(\\theta)=P_\\theta(\\text{reject }H_0)$ is defined over the ENTIRE parameter space: under $H_0$ it equals the (nominal) size, and under $H_1$ it equals the power.","tipsTricks":["Plotting $\\beta(\\theta)$ across the WHOLE parameter space (not just $H_1$) is exactly how size, power, and unbiasedness are all read off one curve.","A common oversight is to think of 'power' as only meaningful under $H_1$ - but the SAME function, evaluated at $H_0$ points, gives the size."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$\\beta(\\theta)=E_\\theta[\\varphi(X)]=P_\\theta(\\text{reject }H_0)$ is well-defined for every $\\theta$ in the parameter space; restricted to $H_0$ it is the size, restricted to $H_1$ it is the power."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-22","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Hypothesis Testing","subtopic":"Neyman-Pearson Lemma and Best Critical Region","question":"$X\\sim N(\\mu,25)$. A sample of size $n=25$ is used to test $H_0:\\mu=50$ against $H_1:\\mu=53$ at $\\alpha=0.05$ (one-sided, using $z_{0.05}=1.645$). The power of the test is approximately","options":["$0.99$","$0.05$","$0.50$","$0.91$"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Numerical","difficulty":"Hard","examShortcut":"Critical value $\\bar x=50+1.645(5/5)=51.645$; power $=P(\\bar X>51.645\\mid\\mu=53)=P(Z>-1.355)\\approx0.91$.","tipsTricks":["$SE=\\sigma/\\sqrt n=5/5=1$ here - always recompute the standard error before converting to a $z$-score.","A NEGATIVE $z$ in the power calculation (as here) means the critical value lies BELOW the true alternative mean, correctly signalling high power."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$SE=5/\\sqrt{25}=1$; critical value $=50+1.645(1)=51.645$."},{"step":2,"text":"Power $=P(\\bar X>51.645\\mid\\mu=53)=P\\!\\left(Z>\\dfrac{51.645-53}{1}\\right)=P(Z>-1.355)=\\Phi(1.355)\\approx0.912$."},{"step":3,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-23","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Hypothesis Testing","subtopic":"UMP, MLR, Unbiased and Similar Tests","question":"For $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ with $\\sigma^2$ known, testing $H_0:\\mu\\le\\mu_0$ against $H_1:\\mu>\\mu_0$, the UMP test rejects $H_0$ when","options":["$|\\bar X-\\mu_0|>z_{\\alpha/2}\\sigma/\\sqrt n$","$\\bar X<\\mu_0-z_\\alpha\\sigma/\\sqrt n$","$\\bar X=\\mu_0$","$\\bar X>\\mu_0+z_\\alpha\\sigma/\\sqrt n$"],"correctAnswer":3,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"The standard one-sided UMP test for a normal mean (variance known) rejects for large $\\bar X$, with the threshold set by the UPPER-tail critical value $z_\\alpha$.","tipsTricks":["Using $z_{\\alpha/2}$ (the two-sided critical value) here would be a mismatch - one-sided alternatives always pair with one-sided ($z_\\alpha$) critical values.","This UMP result follows directly from MLR of the normal family in $\\bar X$ (or equivalently in $\\sum x_i$), via the Karlin-Rubin theorem."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"By the Karlin-Rubin theorem, the normal family (known $\\sigma^2$) has MLR in $\\bar X$, so the UMP test for this one-sided problem rejects for large $\\bar X$: specifically $\\bar X>\\mu_0+z_\\alpha\\sigma/\\sqrt n$."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (d)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-24","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Hypothesis Testing","subtopic":"Likelihood Ratio Test","question":"For testing equality of two normal population variances, $H_0:\\sigma_1^2=\\sigma_2^2$, using independent samples of sizes $n_1,n_2$, the statistic $S_1^2/S_2^2$ under $H_0$ follows","options":["$F_{n_1-1,\\,n_2-1}$","$t_{n_1+n_2-2}$","$\\chi^2_{n_1+n_2-2}$","$N(0,1)$"],"correctAnswer":0,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"The ratio of two independent sample variances (each scaled chi-square) from normal populations with equal true variance follows the $F$ distribution with numerator and denominator df equal to each sample's error df.","tipsTricks":["Always match numerator df to the FIRST sample's variance ($n_1-1$) and denominator df to the SECOND's ($n_2-1$) - swapping them inverts the test statistic's distribution.","This is the standard variance-ratio test underlying, among other things, the preliminary check before choosing pooled vs. Welch two-sample $t$-tests."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"$(n_1-1)S_1^2/\\sigma_1^2\\sim\\chi^2_{n_1-1}$ and $(n_2-1)S_2^2/\\sigma_2^2\\sim\\chi^2_{n_2-1}$, independent; under $H_0:\\sigma_1^2=\\sigma_2^2$, their ratio (each divided by its own df) gives $S_1^2/S_2^2\\sim F_{n_1-1,n_2-1}$."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (a)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"},{"id":"F-HT-25","section":"Hypothesis Testing","topic":"Sequential and Decision Theory","subtopic":"Wald's SPRT, OC and ASN Functions","question":"Under Wald's SPRT, if $\\theta$ is the true parameter value (with $\\theta_0\\ne\\theta_1$ and finite, nonzero boundaries $A,B$), the probability that the procedure eventually terminates with a decision is","options":["$\\alpha$","$1$ - the SPRT terminates with probability $1$","$\\beta$","$0$"],"correctAnswer":1,"questionType":"Conceptual","difficulty":"Medium","examShortcut":"A fundamental SPRT result: as long as $\\theta_0\\ne\\theta_1$, the procedure terminates (reaches a decision) with probability $1$, regardless of the true $\\theta$.","tipsTricks":["This is what makes SPRT a well-defined, always-terminating procedure in practice - sampling is guaranteed to stop eventually, even though the sample size itself is random.","The proof relies on the log-likelihood-ratio random walk having a nonzero drift whenever $\\theta\\ne(\\theta_0+\\theta_1)/2$-type boundary cases are handled appropriately, ensuring it eventually crosses one of the two boundaries."],"solution":[{"step":1,"text":"Wald's theory establishes that the sequential probability ratio random walk crosses one of the two finite boundaries $A,B$ with probability $1$ in finite (expected) time, for any true $\\theta$, whenever $\\theta_0\\ne\\theta_1$."},{"step":2,"text":"Option (b)."}],"explanationSource":"AI-derived explanation","isForecast":true,"provenanceLabel":"FORECAST / AI-GENERATED — NOT AN AUTHENTIC PYQ"}];
